  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nSouthwesterly flow continues across the region as an upper level\nlow digs across Western CONUS. The wave that brought light \nrain through the region is up in Canada now with the wave \nforecast to move up from western South Dakota and eastern \nMontana into North Dakota tonight. Rain is forecast to expand \nwest to east tonight with the heaviest amounts forecast to fall \nin the northwest. With medium chances for half of an inch of \nrain. There is a low chance of snow mixing in Monday morning \nacross the far northwest as temperatures approach freezing. A \ncolder airmass will stay in place through the middle of the week\nresulting in temperatures in the upper 40s to 50s. Overnight \nlow temperatures will likely approach freezing Monday and \nTuesday morning. A hard freezing will be more likely Tuesday \nmorning across the northwest.\n\nThe upper low that is digging out west will cutoff early next\nweek and slowly pass south of the Northern Plains. Temperature\nwill slowly trend up by the end of the week. There is a \nnorthern stream low that will pass northeast of the region \nlikely bring low chances for precipitation and breezy \nconditions. The ENS and GEFS ensembles are favoring this system \nto strengthen over the Great Lakes instead of the Northern \nPlains. After this next system moves east the NBM is favoring \nabove normal temperatures forecast next weekend into early next \nweek.",
